WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.567
You have Glass, congratulations.

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:06.944
When Google Glass came out in 2013, they were ahead of their time.

00:00:06.944 --> 00:00:13.000
But they didn’t last for several reasons.
To do anything, you had to tilt your head and say “ok glass”.

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:19.855
Or use the touchpad. They were also expensive, clunky, and most importantly, people didn’t know why they needed them.

00:00:19.855 --> 00:00:23.847
Google eventually reinvented the glasses as a tool for professionals.

00:00:23.847 --> 00:00:27.833
They’re not sold directly to consumers, they’re not a mainstream product.

00:00:27.833 --> 00:00:31.862
But what’s around the corner is meant to appeal to the masses.

00:00:31.862 --> 00:00:37.219
The small and mighty Apple Watch has marked a trend with Apple’s devices. They’re getting more personal.

00:00:37.219 --> 00:00:44.336
From the iMac to the Macbook. The idea for the iPad led to the iPhone, even though the smartphone technically came out first.

00:00:44.336 --> 00:00:49.727
And then, the smartwatch. The next big product will be the most personal of them all.

00:00:56.621 --> 00:01:01.816
A normal-looking pair of glasses - perhaps like these - is the future of augmented reality.

00:01:01.816 --> 00:01:04.928
AR blends the real world with the virtual one.

00:01:04.928 --> 00:01:12.349
The glasses will bring information from your phone to your face so you’ll be able to see texts, emails, and more on both lenses

00:01:12.349 --> 00:01:15.918
without anyone else around you being able to see what's on your screen.

00:01:15.918 --> 00:01:19.102
The glasses are said to be controlled with gestures.

00:01:19.102 --> 00:01:27.908
A patent also describes how the device could recognize blinking, smiling, or raising your eyebrows by tracking the movement of the attached light sensors. 

00:01:27.908 --> 00:01:33.281
Typically, an augmented reality experience would require cameras to make sense of the surroundings.

00:01:33.281 --> 00:01:38.162
However, the glasses won't come with one due to fears of people being secretly recorded.

00:01:38.162 --> 00:01:43.960
Apple has always taken a strong stance on privacy and saw the backlash when Google included a camera.

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:53.062
But they will still be able to "see" thanks to a revolutionary piece of technology that's already in the new iPhone 12 Pro and the latest iPad Pro.

00:01:54.558 --> 00:01:58.617
A LiDAR scanner, which stands for “light detection and ranging”.

00:01:59.634 --> 00:02:05.233
If you’ve heard of it before it might be due to self-driving cars, which use LiDAR to detect its surroundings.

00:02:05.495 --> 00:02:12.219
The scanner gauges distance and depth by beaming out laser light and measuring the reflection with a sensor.

00:02:12.219 --> 00:02:19.434
This can be leveraged on the iPhone to take better quality photos at night and could be used in the glasses to help us see better in the dark.

00:02:19.434 --> 00:02:25.243
More importantly, LiDAR opens up a world of possibilities for enhancing the AR experience.

00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:30.618
When it first came to the iPad, virtual objects could be placed precisely on top of real ones.

00:02:31.683 --> 00:02:36.461
In the same way, the glasses could overlay directions on top of your view of real life.

00:02:36.461 --> 00:02:43.082
It remains to be seen what standout feature or app using AR will entice people to want to get their hands on a pair. 

00:02:43.082 --> 00:02:48.318
Based on its history, Apple hasn’t had a problem convincing people to buy its products.

00:02:48.318 --> 00:02:56.373
The company’s arguably greatest strength is that customers trust in the quality of what they’re getting, even something as odd-looking as AirPods.

00:02:56.373 --> 00:02:59.948
Just like the AirPods, the glasses will connect to the iPhone.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.966
Since the phone does the processing, the smart glasses won't have as much material packed into them - keeping them slim, lightweight, and less expensive. 

00:03:08.966 --> 00:03:13.857
One of the major problems with Google Glass was that it was $1,500.

00:03:13.857 --> 00:03:20.134
If we look at the first generation of some Apple products that became a smashing success, they were significantly less.

00:03:20.134 --> 00:03:29.765
The iPad launched at $499. The Apple Watch started at $349. So it wouldn’t be surprising for the glasses to be priced around a similar range. 
 

00:03:29.765 --> 00:03:34.420
Those with prescription lenses will cost slightly more - if they’re even needed.

00:03:34.420 --> 00:03:39.511
A patent shows self-correcting lenses automatically adjusting to suit users’ eyes.

00:03:39.511 --> 00:03:47.386
There apparently won’t be a version for sunglasses, for now, as tinted lenses would interfere with reading the information properly on-screen.

00:03:47.386 --> 00:03:52.084
This is just the first-generation model so future ones could come up with a solution.

00:03:52.467 --> 00:03:57.392
And like the first-generation of any product, it may not have the longest battery life. 

00:03:57.392 --> 00:04:02.216
A major selling point would be if it could last a whole waking day, just like the Apple Watch.

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:08.014
It will most certainly be charged wirelessly, as all of Apple’s products move toward a port-free world. 

00:04:08.014 --> 00:04:11.335
So, the big question is, when will they be released?

00:04:11.335 --> 00:04:20.397
Some who have been following the company closely estimate 2022 or 2023 are safe bets, and possibly as early 2021 -

00:04:20.397 --> 00:04:27.767
the same year Facebook plans to release its first pair of smart glasses. The social media giant has partnered with the maker of Ray-Bans. 

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:32.245
These are not the smart glasses but a research tool to help develop the product. 

00:04:32.245 --> 00:04:37.088
Facebook released a video showing the endless ways AR can be helpful in everyday life, from reminding you where you left your keys 

00:04:37.088 --> 00:04:46.364
to pointing out recommendations when you walk into a store. Though it's unclear if the first pair will have all of these functions.

00:04:46.364 --> 00:04:54.819
Facebook has already ventured into the eyewear world. In September, it unveiled the second generation Oculus Quest - a virtual reality headset.

00:04:54.819 --> 00:05:02.214
VR is different from AR because users are taken out of the real-world experience completely and put into a simulated one.

00:05:02.214 --> 00:05:07.615
Apple is believed to be working on a headset as well, reportedly combining both AR and VR.

00:05:07.615 --> 00:05:13.730
t’s meant for gaming, streaming videos, and video conferencing so this will be more of a specialized product

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:18.614
whereas the smart glasses are an everyday wearable meant to appeal to a wider audience.

00:05:18.614 --> 00:05:24.044
Apple is banking on them to finally make augmented reality eyewear mainstream.

00:05:24.568 --> 00:05:26.920
They may not be something we thought we ever needed. 

00:05:27.516 --> 00:05:30.155
They may end up being something that we want.

